we are given with an image file from the challenge 


first i used file command to check the file type then i used exiftool to ch3eck if there are any other useful information , but nothing stood out 

then i used hexeditor to check the hex thats when i came across something familar, i noticed the header was PK

PK encoded into hex is 50 4b, which are the 2 bytes we see first 

looking up i saw that the PKZip archive should start with 50 4b 03 04 

then the hex bytes after 50 4b should be 03 and 04 but in this case it is not 

then i was thinking about first putting every individual byte onto its own line and save it as a file first then remove every other pair of bytes. ie 

as the header was seperate by a pair of bytes i want to try and removing every alternate pair of bytes 

so i made a python script to do it 


bytes = []

fileObj = open("hex", "r")

bytes = fileObj.read().split()

fileObj.close()
 
p1 = bytes[0::4] 
p2 = bytes[1::4] 

zipstring = " "

for f,b in zip(p1,p2):
 zipstring += f
 zipstring += b
 
print(zipstring)

save this file, i named it as script.py

then ran the script on the image using this command 

python3 script.py | xxd -r -p > hex_trunc

hex_trunc is the output file name 

now we extract the content from the hex_trunc file using binwalk 

binwalk -e hex_trunc

this gives us a new folder which has a zip file 

we open this and it gives us a huge text file which seems like a book 

i simply used the search feature to search for the picoCTF flag format and got the flag 

